1
00:00:08,892 --> 00:00:10,542
Who ages and dies?

2
00:00:10,622 --> 00:00:13,196
It sounds like the
simplest question there is.

3
00:00:14,008 --> 00:00:16,636
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

4
00:00:16,716 --> 00:00:20,594
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

5
00:00:21,404 --> 00:00:23,692
Part 1 set out the twelve links.

6
00:00:23,772 --> 00:00:25,921
From ignorance to ageing and death.

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:30,477
And it ended on a question the list does
not answer. Who is turning the wheel?

8
00:00:31,272 --> 00:00:33,764
Here I stay with the
tradition's own answer.

9
00:00:34,564 --> 00:00:36,785
A monk once asked the Buddha directly.

10
00:00:36,865 --> 00:00:39,438
What is ageing and death, and whose is it?

11
00:00:40,256 --> 00:00:42,504
The Buddha did not answer with a name.

12
00:00:42,584 --> 00:00:44,515
Not a valid question, he said.

13
00:00:44,595 --> 00:00:47,708
Whether one says the life
and the body are the same,

14
00:00:47,788 --> 00:00:51,349
or that they are different, there
is no living of the holy life.

15
00:00:51,429 --> 00:00:54,311
Avoiding both, he teaches by the middle.

16
00:00:54,391 --> 00:00:57,345
With birth as condition, ageing and death.

17
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:03,187
And he goes through every link the same
way. Whose is birth? Not a valid question.

18
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:05,827
With becoming as condition, birth.

19
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:09,173
The question asks for
an owner of the chain.

20
00:01:09,253 --> 00:01:12,153
The reply gives the chain, and no owner.

21
00:01:12,956 --> 00:01:15,353
The question comes back in other forms.

22
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:18,279
And the answer is always
shaped the same way.

23
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:23,515
A brahmin asks. Is the one who acts the
same as the one who meets the result?

24
00:01:23,595 --> 00:01:26,151
That is one extreme, the Buddha says.

25
00:01:26,231 --> 00:01:29,358
Is the one who acts someone
else? That is the other.

26
00:01:29,438 --> 00:01:32,120
Avoiding both, he teaches the chain.

27
00:01:32,932 --> 00:01:34,854
A wandering ascetic asks.

28
00:01:34,934 --> 00:01:40,934
Is suffering made by oneself? Not
so. By another? Not so. By both?

29
00:01:41,197 --> 00:01:44,392
Not so. By chance? Not so.

30
00:01:44,472 --> 00:01:46,009
Then is there no suffering?

31
00:01:46,089 --> 00:01:48,305
There is suffering, the Buddha says.

32
00:01:48,385 --> 00:01:51,801
I know it, and I see it.
And he gives the chain.

33
00:01:52,616 --> 00:01:55,271
Each time, the questioner needs a someone.

34
00:01:55,351 --> 00:01:58,709
A self who made the suffering.
Or another who made it.

35
00:01:58,789 --> 00:02:02,038
Or a self who acted, and
the same self who reaps.

36
00:02:02,118 --> 00:02:04,932
Each time, the Buddha
declines the someone,

37
00:02:05,012 --> 00:02:06,634
and gives the conditions.

38
00:02:07,428 --> 00:02:11,388
One short discourse puts it in
a way I find hard to forget.

39
00:02:12,208 --> 00:02:14,116
This body is not yours.

40
00:02:14,196 --> 00:02:16,032
Nor does it belong to others.

41
00:02:16,112 --> 00:02:21,009
It should be seen as old karma, shaped
by intention, something to be felt.

42
00:02:21,812 --> 00:02:24,345
Not mine. Not someone else's.

43
00:02:24,425 --> 00:02:26,523
Old karma, felt now.

44
00:02:27,336 --> 00:02:30,494
This is how the tradition
holds karma without an owner.

45
00:02:30,574 --> 00:02:34,271
Formations, the second link,
are intentional actions.

46
00:02:34,351 --> 00:02:37,682
They shape what comes next,
the way a river cuts its bed.

47
00:02:37,762 --> 00:02:40,240
And what comes next flows along that bed.

48
00:02:41,044 --> 00:02:44,211
There is a link between what
is done and what is met,

49
00:02:44,291 --> 00:02:45,928
reliable and exact.

50
00:02:46,008 --> 00:02:48,591
But there is no one
standing behind the doing,

51
00:02:48,671 --> 00:02:50,334
who then waits for the result.

52
00:02:51,128 --> 00:02:54,662
The classical reading lays the
twelve links over three lives,

53
00:02:54,742 --> 00:02:56,514
and divides them carefully.

54
00:02:57,324 --> 00:03:02,099
Past causes. Ignorance and
formations. Present results.

55
00:03:02,179 --> 00:03:07,153
Consciousness, name and form, the
six senses, contact and feeling.

56
00:03:07,233 --> 00:03:10,998
Present causes. Craving,
clinging and becoming.

57
00:03:11,078 --> 00:03:12,602
And future results.

58
00:03:12,682 --> 00:03:14,943
Birth, and ageing and death.

59
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:18,456
Four groups, joined at three points.

60
00:03:18,536 --> 00:03:20,698
Past action meeting a new life.

61
00:03:20,778 --> 00:03:22,913
What is met turning into what is done.

62
00:03:22,993 --> 00:03:25,634
And present action shaping a life to come.

63
00:03:26,444 --> 00:03:30,780
In this reading, a new life begins with a
moment of consciousness that links it to

64
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:33,637
the last. Nothing travels across.

65
00:03:33,717 --> 00:03:36,790
The tradition's image is
a flame lit from a flame.

66
00:03:36,870 --> 00:03:41,210
The one reborn is neither the same
as the one who died, nor another.

67
00:03:42,024 --> 00:03:45,196
So the three-life reading
keeps rebirth at the centre.

68
00:03:45,276 --> 00:03:47,179
And keeps it without a traveller.

69
00:03:47,259 --> 00:03:49,466
That was worked out with great care.

70
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:52,816
Behind it stands the
work of the Abhidharma,

71
00:03:52,896 --> 00:03:56,394
the schools that set out to
analyse experience completely.

72
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,575
Their achievement was real,
and I want to say so plainly.

73
00:04:00,655 --> 00:04:02,328
They took the person apart.

74
00:04:02,408 --> 00:04:06,268
What we call I turned out to be
a stream of momentary factors.

75
00:04:06,348 --> 00:04:10,426
Moments of contact, feeling,
perception, intention,

76
00:04:10,506 --> 00:04:12,491
consciousness, and matter.

77
00:04:12,571 --> 00:04:15,430
Look for a self among
them, and none is found.

78
00:04:16,236 --> 00:04:17,995
The analysis was thorough.

79
00:04:18,075 --> 00:04:20,671
One school listed four kinds of condition.

80
00:04:20,751 --> 00:04:23,117
The Theravāda counted twenty four.

81
00:04:23,197 --> 00:04:25,889
Each factor was defined by its own mark.

82
00:04:25,969 --> 00:04:27,777
Contact is what touches.

83
00:04:27,857 --> 00:04:29,462
Feeling is what feels.

84
00:04:29,542 --> 00:04:33,271
Each arises, stands for
a moment, and ceases.

85
00:04:34,072 --> 00:04:36,836
So the tradition answers
in two ways at once.

86
00:04:36,916 --> 00:04:39,635
The discourses say, not a valid question.

87
00:04:39,715 --> 00:04:44,064
The Abhidharma says, look, and
you will find only these moments,

88
00:04:44,144 --> 00:04:46,936
linked by these conditions,
and no one among them.

89
00:04:47,732 --> 00:04:50,849
In this picture there is
no self among the pieces.

90
00:04:50,929 --> 00:04:55,102
But the pieces themselves are taken
as real, each with its own nature.

91
00:04:55,182 --> 00:04:58,272
Where I part from that,
the next section will show.

92
00:04:59,088 --> 00:05:02,042
This is where the teaching
is most often misheard.

93
00:05:02,122 --> 00:05:03,731
So I want to be plain.

94
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:07,372
No owner does not mean no responsibility.

95
00:05:07,452 --> 00:05:11,285
Kind acts tend toward happiness,
and cruel acts toward suffering.

96
00:05:11,365 --> 00:05:13,214
Practice changes a person.

97
00:05:13,294 --> 00:05:15,728
One is responsible for what one has done.

98
00:05:15,808 --> 00:05:20,515
The conventional I keeps its promises,
and meets the results of what it has done.

99
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:25,823
The twelve links keep karma, rebirth,
and the path that ends suffering.

100
00:05:25,903 --> 00:05:28,110
What is left out is only the owner.

101
00:05:28,908 --> 00:05:31,441
And one more thing, equally plain.

102
00:05:31,521 --> 00:05:35,096
That rebirth needs no traveller
removes one objection to it.

103
00:05:35,176 --> 00:05:39,145
But removing a bad argument against
something is not the same as giving a good

104
00:05:39,225 --> 00:05:44,163
argument for it. Whether rebirth happens
is a matter for its own evidence.

105
00:05:44,968 --> 00:05:48,112
The next time I am hurt, I
can try the monk's question.

106
00:05:48,192 --> 00:05:51,813
Not as philosophy, but
as looking. Who is hurt?

107
00:05:52,628 --> 00:05:56,696
Not to deny the hurt. It
is there, sharp and vivid.

108
00:05:56,776 --> 00:05:58,680
But to look for the one it belongs to.

109
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:03,762
What I find is the words, the feeling,
the tightening, the story starting up.

110
00:06:03,842 --> 00:06:05,460
Each is fully present.

111
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:08,549
The owner, looked for, is not found.

112
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,564
This is Section 5, Part 2 of
the Dependent Arising series.

113
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:18,340
And when the owner is not found,
the story has less to stand on.

